Jose eventually even worked at Gould Farms Roadside Diner, which is another thing that's really unique about this place. So at the diner, guests are servers and cooks. They are dealing with the public, topping off coffees, selling homemade bagels in these big gigantic pancakes. And you can, I think, really hear an echo back to Kirkbride in that idea of giving people meaningful work, you know, helping someone feel like they're competent and useful. It's so unlike the mental health care status quo where, you know, people are being shut up in

67
00:34:16.000 --> 00:34:45.000
some institutional building, told to wear pajamas all day in play cards. You know, here instead, you've got people who are presently receiving residential psychiatric treatment being given this tremendous challenge of running a restaurant and they are totally kicking butt at it. And so what's up with Jose now? Is he still a guest at Gold Farm? He's actually today he's a staff member at the farm and he's also what's called a certified peer specialist. So he's someone who's done this training and he has this firsthand experience

68
00:34:45.000 --> 00:35:02.000
with psychiatric diagnosis being in treatment, that kind of stuff. And he's lending that expertise to other people who are presently figuring out their own recoveries. So Jose talked to me about how he used to feel like such an outsider. But now he's part of that community. And it's not just the gold farm community,

69
00:35:02.000 --> 00:35:31.000
but the wider community. There's this relationship between the farm and the town, which also feels very Kirkbright you know, locals have histories with the farm. They've had family and friends who've stayed there, worked there. I mean, some people even just go by the cafe for their morning coffee. There's a way in which the town's history is really intertwined with the farm's history and the farm's histories intertwined with the farm sister and the farm sister is intertwined with the town. And I think it matters that people who are in psychiatric treatment and those who aren't

70
00:35:31.000 --> 00:36:15.000
are integrated, you know, that we have opportunities to be reminded of one another's humanity. I think it matters whether people who need mental health care have access to tools like supportive environments, opportunities to grow, to work, to socialize, to be outside. So in a way what we do here is as old as time and it's also the case that we know through contemporary research and studies that what we do here is important and makes a difference. So in the scheme of things, how rare is a place like Guld Farm? In America, really, really rare. You know, there's Guld Farm and a few others like it, some of which were directly based on Guld.

71
00:36:15.000 --> 00:36:37.000
We, the taxpayers, do spend a lot of money on mental health care, but it is not going towards studying or funding approaches like this. We don't receive any government funding, and there's really no insurance reimbursements for people in a residential program. They're a nonprofit and therefore a reliant on constant fundraising. This is expensive care to provide. That said, the folks I met at Gould

72
00:36:37.000 --> 00:37:02.000
seemed really committed to their path. It isn't necessarily easy, but they do feel strongly it's right. And I'm not gonna lie, when with a situation regarding mental health care in America right now can feel pretty hopeless. So when I get to eat something as special as a piece of gold farm cheddar, I'm glad to feel a little bit of hope. Well, thanks so much, Sandy. Thank you.

